<body>

<div class="container">

    <h1 class="exam-title">Histology of Bone Tissue <span class="badge-answered">ANSWERED KEY VERSION</span></h1>

    <div class="page">
        <div class="page-header">Page 1: General Characters, Growth Dynamics, Periosteum & Endosteum</div>
        
        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q1. Histologically, how is bone tissue distinct from cartilage tissue regarding its blood supply and physical consistency?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Bone is avascular and rigid, while cartilage is highly vascular and hard.</li>
                <li>B) Both tissues are completely avascular but bone has a harder calcified matrix.</li>
                <td class="correct">C) Bone is highly vascular and hard, while cartilage is avascular and rigid.</td>
                <li>D) Bone is avascular and hard, while cartilage is highly vascular and rigid.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q2. During bone remodeling and development, appositional growth is responsible for increasing which of the following skeletal dimensions?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <td class="correct">A) Bone width</td>
                <li>B) Bone length</li>
                <li>C) Total number of osteons</li>
                <li>D) Medullary cavity length</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q3. A researcher examines a section of the outer fibrous layer of the periosteum. Which group of histological components should be most abundant?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Osteogenic cells, osteoblasts, and elastic fibers</li>
                <li>B) Single layer of flattened cells with minimal extracellular matrix</li>
                <li>C) Loose connective tissue entirely lacking blood vessels</li>
                <td class="correct">D) Dense collagen fibers, fibroblasts, and many blood vessels</td>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q4. Which statement perfectly describes the nature and role of Sharpey's fibers in bone histology?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) They are type II collagen fibers secreted by chondrocytes to line the endosteum.</li>
                <td class="correct">B) They are penetrating type I collagen fibers from the outer periosteum anchoring it to the bone matrix.</td>
                <li>D) They are elastic fibers running exclusively inside the central Haversian canals.</li>
                <li>D) They are specialized cytoplasmic extensions linking osteoclasts together.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q5. Histologically, the endosteum is characterized as:</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) A thick, calcified outer protective layer containing dense fibrous networks.</li>
                <li>B) A multi-layered structure of active multinucleated giant cells.</li>
                <td class="correct">C) A single layer of flattened osteogenic cells and osteoblasts lining internal cavities.</td>
                <li>D) An avascular sheath covering the articular ends of long bones.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q6. The organic component of the bone matrix (osteoid) constitutes approximately 35% of its dry weight. Which molecule forms more than 90% of this organic framework?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <td class="correct">A) Collagen fibers type I</td>
                <li>B) Collagen fibers type II</li>
                <li>C) Chondroitin and keratan sulphate</li>
                <li>D) Osteocalcin and osteopontin</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="answer-key">Page 1 Answer Key: Q1: C | Q2: A | Q3: D | Q4: B | Q5: C | Q6: A</div>
    </div>


    <div class="page">
        <div class="page-header">Page 2: Bone Matrix Components & Bone Cells I (Osteogenic & Osteoblasts)</div>
        
        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q7. Which inorganic mineral combination is primarily responsible for the characteristic hardness and rigidity of the bone matrix, making up 65% of its dry weight?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Bicarbonate and Citrate</li>
                <li>B) Sodium and Potassium</li>
                <li>C) Magnesium and Proteoglycans</li>
                <td class="correct">D) Calcium and Phosphorus</td>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q8. Which of the following is the ONLY type of bone cell that retains the biological capacity to undergo mitotic cell division?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Osteoblast</li>
                <td class="correct">B) Osteogenic cell</td>
                <li>C) Osteocyte</li>
                <li>D) Osteoclast</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q9. Embryonically, osteogenic cells directly develop from which specific cell lineage?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Hematopoietic stem cells</li>
                <li>B) Monocyte precursor cells</li>
                <td class="correct">C) Undifferentiated mesenchymal cells (UMCs)</td>
                <li>D) Fully differentiated chondroblasts</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q10. Under the light microscope, active bone-forming osteoblasts display a deeply basophilic cytoplasm and PAS-positive granules. This histochemical characteristic is correlated with their rich production of:</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <td class="correct">A) Alkaline phosphatase</td>
                <li>B) Acid phosphatase</li>
                <li>C) Collagenase</li>
                <li>D) Carbonic anhydrase</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q12. Active osteoblasts are structurally interconnected with neighboring cells on the bone surface via processes containing which type of intercellular junction?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Desmosomes</li>
                <li>B) Tight junctions</li>
                <li>C) Zonula occludens</li>
                <td class="correct">D) Gap junctions</td>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q12. In addition to synthesizing type I collagen and bone proteins, osteoblasts produce multiple hematopoietic cytokines. Which of the following is NOT synthesized by osteoblasts?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Granulocyte-colony stimulating factor (G-CSF)</li>
                <td class="correct">B) Acid phosphatase lysosomal packets</td>
                <li>C) Macrophage-colony stimulating factor (M-CSF)</li>
                <li>D) Interleukins</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="answer-key">Page 2 Answer Key: Q7: D | Q8: B | Q9: C | Q10: A | Q11: D | Q12: B</div>
    </div>


    <div class="page">
        <div class="page-header">Page 3: Bone Cells II (Osteocytes & Osteoclasts)</div>
        
        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q13. When an active osteoblast becomes completely surrounded by its newly synthesized mineralized matrix, it transforms into which mature cell type?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Osteogenic stem cell</li>
                <li>B) Multinucleated osteoclast</li>
                <td class="correct">C) Mature osteocyte</td>
                <li>D) Fibroblast</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q14. In contrast to chondrocytes in cartilage, mature osteocytes residing within the bone matrix:</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <td class="correct">A) Present singly inside individual lacunae and cannot divide to form cell nests.</td>
                <li>B) Form large isogenous cell nests inside irregular lacunae.</li>
                <li>C) Retain a high mitotic rate resulting in rapid interstitial bone growth.</li>
                <li>D) Completely lack dendritic cytoplasmic processes.</td>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q15. Through which microscopic channels do the dendritic processes of osteocytes extend to communicate via gap junctions and receive nutrients?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Volkmann's canals</li>
                <li>B) Haversian canals</li>
                <li>C) Howship's lacunae</li>
                <td class="correct">D) Canaliculi</td>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q16. Osteoclasts are specialized motile bone-resorbing cells. From which cellular lineage do functional osteoclasts originate?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Mitotic division of inner osteoblasts</li>
                <td class="correct">B) Bone marrow precursors in common with monocytes</td>
                <li>C) Undifferentiated mesenchymal cells lining the periosteum</li>
                <li>D) Direct transformation of aging osteocytes</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q17. Under the light microscope, osteoclasts are easily recognized because they are:</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <td class="correct">A) Large, irregular multinucleated giant cells with acidophilic cytoplasm sitting in Howship's lacunae.</td>
                <li>B) Small, elongated spindle-shaped cells with pale basophilic cytoplasm.</li>
                <li>C) Cuboidal, single-nucleated cells arranged side-by-side on growing bone surfaces.</li>
                <li>D) Mononucleated cells exhibiting intense cytoplasmic PAS positivity and no processes.</td>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q18. Lysosomes are highly abundant in the osteoclast cytoplasm, imparting strong histochemical positivity for which specific enzyme marker?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Alkaline phosphatase</li>
                <li>B) Carbonic anhydrase</li>
                <td class="correct">C) Acid phosphatase</td>
                <li>D) Hyaluronidase</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="answer-key">Page 3 Answer Key: Q13: C | Q14: A | Q15: D | Q16: B | Q17: A | Q18: C</div>
    </div>


    <div class="page">
        <div class="page-header">Page 4: Osteoclast Ultrastructure, Tissue Preparation & Histological Maturity</div>
        
        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q19. Under Transmission Electron Microscopy (TEM), the osteoclast is divided into 4 zones. Which zone lacks major organelles, contains actin microfilaments, and acts as the sealing zone against the bone matrix?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Ruffled border</li>
                <li>B) Vesicular zone</li>
                <li>C) Basal zone</li>
                <td class="correct">D) Clear zone</td>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q20. During bone resorption, how do osteoclasts achieve the dissolution and decalcification of the inorganic calcium salts within the matrix?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) By secreting alkaline phosphatase inside the compartments</li>
                <td class="correct">B) By secreting excess H+ ions to produce an acidic microenvironment</td>
                <li>C) By producing large quantities of type II collagen fibrils</li>
                <li>D) By executing phagocytosis of entire intact osteons</td>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q21. To prepare a bone section for standard histological staining (H&E), a specimen is placed in a nitric acid solution or EDTA. What is the fundamental mechanism and result of this decalcified bone technique?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <td class="correct">A) It removes calcium salts to render the bone soft enough for routine sectioning.</td>
                <li>B) It destroys all organic components leaving only hard inorganic minerals.</li>
                <li>C) It prevents air from entering the lacunae so cells appear completely black.</li>
                <li>D) It preserves the exact mineralization rate without altering cell morphology.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q22. During the microscopic examination of a ground bone section prepared by sawing dry bone into thin slices without staining, the lacunae appear as sharp black spaces. What is the cause of this appearance?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Silver nitrate dyes precipitate selectively within active osteocyte nuclei.</li>
                <li>B) Active osteoblasts fill the spaces with dark, calcified mineral pigments.</li>
                <td class="correct">C) The cells are destroyed during grinding, leaving air-filled spaces that scatter light.</td>
                <li>D) Intact osteoclasts accumulate iron and potassium inside their compartments.</td>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q23. Which of the following is a primary feature distinguishing primary (immature or woven) bone from secondary (mature or lamellar) bone?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Primary bone contains significantly more mineral content than secondary bone.</li>
                <td class="correct">B) Primary bone exhibits an irregular or random arrangement of collagen fibers.</td>
                <li>C) Primary bone is organized into fully developed Haversian systems.</li>
                <li>D) Primary bone never appears during the healing of adult bone fractures.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q24. In adults, primary (immature) bone tissue is normally replaced by mature bone. In which specific anatomical area does primary bone persist permanently?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Shaft of long bones</li>
                <li>B) Outer and inner tables of flat skull bones</li>
                <li>C) Diploë layer of the sternum</li>
                <td class="correct">D) Tooth alveoli and insertion sites of some tendons</td>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="answer-key">Page 4 Answer Key: Q19: D | Q20: B | Q21: A | Q22: C | Q23: B | Q24: D</div>
    </div>


    <div class="page">
        <div class="page-header">Page 5: Structural Classification, Lamellar Systems & Vascular Canals</div>
        
        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q25. Where is compact cortical bone tissue normally found within the human skeletal system?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <td class="correct">A) Shafts (diaphyses) of long bones and inner/outer tables of flat skull bones.</td>
                <li>B) Epiphyses (ends) of long bones exclusively.</li>
                <li>C) Deep interior core of short and irregular bones only.</li>
                <li>D) Lining the central core of all embryonic bone trabeculae.</td>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q26. What is the term used for the thin layer of spongy bone enclosed between the inner and outer tables of compact bone in the skull?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Cement line</li>
                <li>B) Osteon core</li>
                <td class="correct">C) Diploë</td>
                <li>D) Trabecular matrix</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q27. The basic structural and functional repeating unit of mature compact bone is the Haversian system (osteon). Each fully developed osteon consists of:</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Thin interconnected rods called trabeculae with large irregular marrow spaces.</li>
                <td class="correct">B) A cylinder of 4 to 20 concentric bone lamellae arranged around a central canal.</td>
                <li>C) Parallel arrays of non-calcified type II collagen fibers without cells.</li>
                <li>D) A single large multi-nucleated osteoclast enclosing a network of canaliculi.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q28. What does the central Haversian canal of an osteon contain histologically?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Fluid-filled cartilage matrix completely lacking cells or nerves</li>
                <li>B) Pure dense irregular connective tissue without any blood vessels</li>
                <li>C) Highly calcified crystalline plates of mature hydroxyapatite</li>
                <td class="correct">D) Loose connective tissue rich in blood vessels, nerves, and an osteogenic cell lining</td>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q29. A student observes vascular channels running perpendicular or obliquely to the long axis of the bone, connecting Haversian canals to each other and to the periosteum. These channels are not surrounded by concentric lamellae. What are they?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <td class="correct">A) Volkmann's canals</td>
                <li>B) Canaliculi</li>
                <li>C) Central canals</li>
                <li>D) Howship's pathways</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q30. Which system represents the angular fragments of old, partially resorbed osteons located between fully intact Haversian systems in compact bone?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Outer circumferential lamellae</li>
                <li>B) Inner circumferential lamellae</li>
                <td class="correct">C) Interstitial lamellae</td>
                <li>D) Trabecular lamellae</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="answer-key">Page 5 Answer Key: Q25: A | Q26: C | Q27: B | Q28: D | Q29: A | Q30: C</div>
    </div>


    <div class="page">
        <div class="page-header">Page 6: Advanced Architectural Details & Hormonal Regulation (New Additions)</div>
        
        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q31. How do the thin bony trabeculae of spongy (cancellous) bone primarily receive their nutrition given the complete absence of true Haversian systems?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Through deep Volkmann's canals that perforate the outer periosteum.</li>
                <td class="correct">B) By diffusion from blood vessels located in the adjacent bone marrow cavity.</td>
                <li>C) Via direct connection to the main nutrient artery of the diaphysis.</li>
                <li>D) By active transport through non-calcified type II collagen channels.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q32. Which specialized histological structure marks the outermost boundary of a fully formed osteon, separating it from interstitial lamellae?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Sharpey's border</li>
                <li>B) Osteoid line</li>
                <td class="correct">C) Cement line</td>
                <li>D) Canalicular junction</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q33. What is the primary functional significance of the ruffled border (ruffled border zone) in an active osteoclast under TEM?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <td class="correct">A) To vastly increase the surface area for secreting protons (H+) and lysosomal enzymes.</td>
                <li>B) To anchor the cell firmly to the matrix using specialized desmosomes.</li>
                <li>C) To store large numbers of glycogen granules for anaerobic metabolism.</td>
                <li>D) To undergo rapid mitotic division during active bone growth.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q34. How does Parathyroid Hormone (PTH) physiologically stimulate bone resorption by osteoclasts, considering that osteoclasts lack direct PTH receptors?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) It binds directly to monocytes causing immediate fusion into giant cells.</li>
                <td class="correct">B) It binds to osteoblasts, stimulating them to release RANKL which activates osteoclastogenesis.</td>
                <li>C) It destroys osteoprotegerin (OPG) within the blood plasma directly.</td>
                <li>D) It lowers the pH of the entire systemic circulation to dissolve hydroxyapatite.</td>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q35. Which hormone acts directly on osteoclasts via specific cell-surface receptors to rapidly inhibit their bone-resorbing activity and lower blood calcium levels?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Growth Hormone</li>
                <li>B) Parathyroid Hormone (PTH)</li>
                <li>C) Thyroxine</li>
                <td class="correct">D) Calcitonin</td>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q36. Which non-collagenous structural glycoprotein within the organic bone matrix plays a critical role in binding calcium and anchoring collagen fibers to the inorganic hydroxyapatite crystals?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Chondroitin sulfate</li>
                <td class="correct">B) Osteonectin</td>
                <li>C) Fibronectin</li>
                <li>D) Hyaluronic acid</td>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="question">
            <div class="question-text">Q37. In a decalcified bone section stained with Haematoxylin and Eosin (H&E), which of the following tissue components is completely lost and cannot be visualized?</div>
            <ul class="options">
                <li>A) Osteocyte networks inside lacunae</li>
                <li>B) Type I collagen fibers of the osteoid</li>
                <td class="correct">C) Inorganic calcium hydroxyapatite crystals</td>
                <li>D) Fibrous periosteal connective tissue layers</td>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="answer-key">Page 6 Answer Key: Q31: B | Q32: C | Q33: A | Q34: B | Q35: D | Q36: B | Q37: C</div>
    </div>

</div>

</body>
